Queues flow the FIFO -> First in First Out and LILO -> Last in Last out principle

In Queue have rear and front
rear -- push --> pichhe
front ---> pop || peek ---> aage

In Java, you cannot instantiate a Queue like this:
Queue<Integer> q = new Queue<>();
because Queue is an interface, not a class, and you cannot create objects from an interface directly.

stack               queue
pop                  remove , poll
peek                 peek , element
push                 add


Types of queue:
1) Queue
2) Deque
3) Circular Queue


## Advantages of Linked list implementation of queue over array implementation
1) Unlimited size
2)  LL works like a queue

## Disadvantages
1) size --> LL has two members


###Circular queue
ArrayImplementation ---< limitation ---< fixed sized
1. Add (enqueue) elements till the last index — rear goes to the end.
2. Remove (dequeue) a few elements from the front — front moves ahead.
3. But now even though the front part of the array is "empty", you cannot add new elements because rear has reached the end — this causes "false full" condition.

### Deque in Java Collection
Doubly Ended Queue

